winewayland: don't call update_client_surfaces() while holding win_data_mutex

STATUS: active. Fixes a HARD AB-BA DEADLOCK between the app's main thread and winewayland's own
event thread, hit on a FRACTIONAL-SCALE output (measured on Hyprland, 3840x2160 @ scale 1.5).
Symptom: the game maps a window that never paints — a plain white/blank surface, zero CPU, no crash,
no log line after the last one the engine flushed. It is a RACE, so it strikes intermittently:
kerbal-space-program hung on 3 of 4 launches on this display, three of them caught live with the
identical mutex-owner pair below; the 4th launch reached its main menu normally. Both of the
symptoms KSP had been reported with — "crashes while loading assets" and "just a white screen" —
are this one hang landing at two different window operations (the MAINMENU scene change, and the
D3D11 swapchain's vkCreateWin32SurfaceKHR). Neither was ever a crash: there is no exception, no
Unity crash dump and no coredump, only a truncated log, which is what a hung writer looks like.

MEASURED, not inferred (KSP 1.12.5 on x86_64-linux, wine-hangover 11.15, DXVK; pthread mutex owners
read straight out of /proc/<pid>/mem at the statics' addresses, threads located with eu-stack):

  user_mutex     (win32u/sysparams.c)      __owner=3325059 __count=2   <- app MAIN thread
  win_data_mutex (winewayland.drv/window.c) __owner=3325467 __count=1  <- wayland EVENT thread
  surfaces_lock  (win32u/window.c)          __owner=3325467

  TID 3325059 (main), holds user_mutex, BLOCKED on win_data_mutex:
    win32u_vkCreateWin32SurfaceKHR -> set_window_pixel_format -> update_window_state
      -> apply_window_pos                 [get_win_ptr  -> user_lock #1]
      -> update_surface_region            [get_win_ptr  -> user_lock #2]
      -> window_surface_set_shape -> window_surface_flush
      -> wayland_window_surface_flush -> set_window_surface_contents
      -> wayland_win_data_get             *** waits for win_data_mutex ***

  TID 3325467 (wayland event thread), holds win_data_mutex + surfaces_lock, BLOCKED on user_mutex:
    waylanddrv_unix_read_events -> wl_display_dispatch_queue -> dispatch_event -> wl_closure_invoke
      -> wp_fractional_scale_handle_scale [wayland_win_data_get -> win_data_mutex]
      -> update_client_surfaces           [surfaces_lock]
      -> NtUserGetAncestor -> list_window_parents -> get_user_handle_ptr
      -> user_lock                        *** waits for user_mutex ***

WHY A FRACTIONAL SCALE IS THE PRECONDITION, and not just background colour: wayland_surface_create()
initialises `surface->window.scale = 1.0`, and wp_fractional_scale_handle_scale() returns EARLY when
the compositor's preferred scale equals the one already recorded. On an unscaled (or integer-scaled)
output the event therefore never reaches update_client_surfaces() and the inverted order is never
taken. Only a scale the surface does not already have — i.e. a fractional one, or a move between
differently-scaled outputs — runs the rest of the function. That is why this looks like a per-host
bug rather than a per-game one: same wine, same game, no deadlock on a scale-1.0 display.

Root cause: win32u OWNS the lock order. It calls into the driver (pWindowSurfaceFlush and friends)
while holding the USER lock, so every driver path is entered as user_mutex -> win_data_mutex.
wp_fractional_scale_handle_scale() ran the opposite way round: it took win_data_mutex in
wayland_win_data_get() and then called update_client_surfaces(), a win32u entry point that takes the
USER lock. When the compositor's wp_fractional_scale_v1.preferred_scale event lands while the app
thread is inside a window-surface flush, the two orders meet and neither side can proceed. Nothing
times out — both are plain pthread_mutex_lock waits — so the process sits there forever.

Fix: hoist update_client_surfaces() out of the locked region, next to the NtUserExposeWindowSurface()
call that already observes this discipline. Everything that reads or writes `data`/`surface` stays
under the lock; only the win32u call moves. update_client_surfaces() -> wayland_client_surface_update()
re-acquires win_data_mutex itself per client surface, so it needs no caller-held lock (it was
previously relying on that mutex being PTHREAD_MUTEX_RECURSIVE).

NOT FIXED HERE, and deliberately: window.c's wayland_win_data_create_wayland_surface() has the same
inverted order (update_client_surfaces() at the `role` change, under win_data_mutex). It is reached
from WAYLAND_WindowPosChanged, i.e. on the app's own thread after win32u has already dropped the USER
lock, and it has never been observed to deadlock. Hoisting it is not a safe local edit — it sits
between `data->wayland_surface = NULL` and wayland_surface_destroy(), where the detach must happen
before the surface is freed. Left alone rather than guessed at.

diff --git i/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_surface.c w/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_surface.c
--- i/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_surface.c
+++ w/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_surface.c
@@ -157,9 +157,6 @@
 
     surface->window.scale = scale;
 
-    /* reattach client surfaces as their rects have changed */
-    update_client_surfaces(hwnd);
-
     /* the subsurface rect has changed */
     if (surface->role == WAYLAND_SURFACE_ROLE_SUBSURFACE)
     {
@@ -169,6 +166,16 @@
 
     wayland_win_data_release(data);
 
+    /* Reattach client surfaces as their rects have changed. This MUST run with win_data_mutex
+     * RELEASED: update_client_surfaces() is a win32u entry point that takes surfaces_lock and then
+     * the USER lock (NtUserGetAncestor -> get_user_handle_ptr -> user_lock), while win32u itself
+     * calls INTO this driver holding the USER lock (apply_window_pos -> update_surface_region ->
+     * window_surface_set_shape -> window_surface_flush -> wayland_window_surface_flush ->
+     * wayland_win_data_get). Taking win_data_mutex before user_mutex here inverts that order and
+     * deadlocks. Same discipline as NtUserExposeWindowSurface below, and as WAYLAND_WindowPosChanged's
+     * is_window_managed() call, which is already hoisted out of the lock for a related reason. */
+    update_client_surfaces(hwnd);
+
     NtUserExposeWindowSurface(hwnd, 0, NULL);
 }
 
